Suppose $RR$ were countable, and list the reals as $r_1, r_2, r_3, ...$. Write each $r_n$ in decimal form:
$
  r_1 & = n_1.d_(11) d_(12) d_(13) d_(14)...   r_2 & = n_2.d_(21) d_(22) d_(23) d_(24)...   r_3 & = n_3.d_(31) d_(32) d_(33) d_(34)... $
Define $r = 0.d_1 d_2 d_3...$ by $d_n = 1$ if $d_(n n) != 1$, and $d_n = 2$ if $d_(n n) = 1$. Then $r$ has only one decimal representation and differs from each $r_n$ at the $n$th decimal place, so no list exhausts $RR$. This is known as *Cantor's diagonal argument*.
